wasm/tools — offline tooling
============================

gen_instr_toml.py — reference interpreter -> spec/instructions.toml.
    Its inputs are the spec authors' OCaml reference interpreter (the
    WebAssembly/spec repo's interpreter/ sources: decode.ml for opcodes and
    immediate shapes, eval*.ml for trap messages, valid.ml as a typing
    cross-check) joined on opcode with the spec PDF's §7.10 instruction
    index for the canonical text mnemonics and Type column — so the table
    comes from the reference implementation, not from anyone's reading of
    the prose. Neither input lives in this tree: fetch both when
    re-deriving. Run once, offline; instructions.toml is a committed
    artifact, deliberately not a build step (regenerating it is a
    spec-version decision, not something a build should do silently).

gen_wasm_ops.py — instructions.toml -> the compiler's src/gen/wasm_ops.h.
    A real build step, driven from compiler/Makefile: the EMIT-side authority
    for each instruction's (prefix, opcode) encoding.

gen_wat_mnemonics.py — instructions.toml -> src/gen/wat_mnemonics.h.
    A real build step: the TEXT-side authority mapping a mnemonic to its opcode,
    shape, natural alignment and operand index spaces. Generated so the .wat
    reader needs no data file at runtime and `water` is a standalone binary.

gen_trap_reasons.c — instructions.toml + spec/wasm.def -> src/gen/jav_trap_reason.h.
    A real build step: it fails the build if wasm.def names a trap reason the
    spec table does not carry.
